#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "5ff4ed47-dffe-534e-baf9-41bded65d457" :question/math-academy-id "e-12385" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/bd504e1d8d85a2f2bbb4b75bd6825120a6aef15345c704714931b6270f84796f.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, \\: 0, \\: 1 + xyz \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/977d93ad65c9f3690def4242f98f4de5e5c89faf02fe47d06f5ed251d8a4a292.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =1+xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}1+xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(1-0)(1-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "06cccfa3-4b70-5e67-9e9f-9f5c83b319bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12386" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 2x,\\: 2y+xz,\\: 2z-xy\\rangle$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\langle x, y, z\\rangle}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle x, y, z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2x,\\,2y+xz,\\,2z-xy⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+(2y+xz)\\cdot y+(2z-xy)\\cdot z \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+xyz+2{z}^{2}-xyz \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "8d046f27-886d-5342-8868-e2c3ae731fa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12453" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/925b2f032f751e4482632c449fff35f544929f3355655c20d6701e640a7b20a3.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c92f6538-7b16-5ae6-956f-7222feae3a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194070} {:answer/id #uuid "f5545bb8-4794-53db-8215-f7eda3020a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194071} {:answer/id #uuid "401c08d4-2de4-533d-8a7b-6a0c449f3b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194072} {:answer/id #uuid "57fffa5e-e7a8-597f-8aec-bcc2a6d82424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194073} {:answer/id #uuid "7882f132-5bd9-5109-b690-8eb3ee1f382c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7882f132-5bd9-5109-b690-8eb3ee1f382c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194074} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ffc3dd-c163-5c41-a448-a318e098e492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "300078ae-d2f8-55e0-bd83-9b0b9c3615b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161162" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8901e7155a476f0019b6d66e3b62b579b91daf99a448e497c93efce1d94415b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8fa71e16d84eb92506e9b10269182f907cb4f2437d0a9bdd3822da48c4cbba4a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a68475-f1e3-5976-abba-f10554b133c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194088} {:answer/id #uuid "988d6513-e622-5dc5-b512-c3009e271f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194089} {:answer/id #uuid "db43ea92-5162-50b4-99ae-c661f782f021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194090} {:answer/id #uuid "16131c65-34e4-5871-931e-47bf7420a20e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194091} {:answer/id #uuid "54cd2d6d-5af9-53d5-900e-c368400ce181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16131c65-34e4-5871-931e-47bf7420a20e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194091} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d650a8a-8a30-59f6-b9cf-6ecf370b38eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc3302a-cfaa-5401-9068-d86cc83df0fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161171" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/c7b22b03b9952bfe068e9252f7336568aa8d761c32a1bc31c444ebb97ee925ba.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e308bfe2-5810-5917-b05f-46e3b09fae20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356410} {:answer/id #uuid "e7da82d8-9fad-59a0-b8d7-8c3b80585b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356411} {:answer/id #uuid "304282f3-c09c-5ccd-b5e0-a5c25254cafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356412} {:answer/id #uuid "8800fea3-4b52-5beb-ba79-65c605cb8195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356413} {:answer/id #uuid "e2463575-e0da-53de-b132-9ea7e3d21908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2463575-e0da-53de-b132-9ea7e3d21908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356414} :answer-field/id #uuid "00155215-0cf8-5178-a478-adc4ce9a60df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e99a63d6-85db-57c1-a93f-e6bbce6a1a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-161203" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/cece4e226becb315d6d951a8ae782750da79209d9a67ec52a8cd4d134999e9e6.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/21e29d99d2d5330db2d946d981e4bd0f820ce26297bf95f962cb3000f0998136.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}(4-{r}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[2{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot (18-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-18\\pi +0 \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41351075-addf-5141-9223-092883a61daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi (4-\\sqrt{2})}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356417} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6d4b4d-880b-505b-bb4d-bf86c098d543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356418} {:answer/id #uuid "44d6048b-42a0-55a8-ba9e-0db014cfe59e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356419} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa73937-3b69-51b4-ad0a-0085efc4882d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356420} {:answer/id #uuid "257cb0de-f26f-5b95-9caa-238efa8a0e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfa73937-3b69-51b4-ad0a-0085efc4882d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356420} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5b31ff-07aa-50c1-96ca-855d539c9f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44feadc1-717a-531e-806e-c3694e9faa4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161207" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/4cb2c8128f24adddacd82b5f477848faa47ae52f95a5bb0636a8b286a6628a08.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/e6fe01196298c1da0961fd01c73ee4cb7c71b6a03a027cceaba0e68be944c968.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{2}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=16-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 16 & 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{16}^{12}\\sqrt{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{12}^{16}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{12}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}[64-24\\sqrt{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c21e84-cad1-5afc-a480-464554a3775a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356424} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e34c0a-0cba-5d14-ae73-4fe7b1c40735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356425} {:answer/id #uuid "417fcacf-cc12-54dc-8afa-e4ea27a7902a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi ({e}^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356426} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee145b9-66e6-5ad9-b1ca-376c5a5b6a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi {e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356427} {:answer/id #uuid "543850c2-b850-5658-a884-1e0103c57402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi (e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543850c2-b850-5658-a884-1e0103c57402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi (e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356428} :answer-field/id #uuid "90730a3e-9170-502f-b913-b12672ae3df3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d33b1813-19d4-55f6-95b6-51d05e1556c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161215" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/534c80a8318ebfe21ba3f21f04ae145f999d6d83c34a402294c789bb2090e014.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/7f44bc35687020594b0d26a2e25b9dbd2b8d75c964a90184626afad6afc1b1e8.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1)+0 \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af55a70-cc2b-5174-8861-2343947ebbc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356431} {:answer/id #uuid "3d519f36-86d1-5617-a667-6af0dcda70e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356432} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebf7d42-f2f5-5013-9701-afc9eb23bd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356433} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f506db-7f8a-5277-971b-5b0cd94791f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356434} {:answer/id #uuid "6183ccc9-771e-53e2-9084-8926f3833a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d519f36-86d1-5617-a667-6af0dcda70e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356432} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f130bb-29da-5102-aa6a-f0d593c640ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d55d2d95-4e58-5273-aa64-8c91074fb49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de659d8-2b59-5729-8169-aa12c9aaa793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356438} {:answer/id #uuid "f1451f77-7a53-575d-a83f-94e1c4bdde7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c8354e-4450-5e6a-bda2-3fa56f5d5862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356440} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5436ec-6401-5db9-856f-2e06b5ea8968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356441} {:answer/id #uuid "21498755-a316-5169-b4ae-449982b36759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21498755-a316-5169-b4ae-449982b36759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356442} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b8a07e-7868-5ac1-a467-acba378903f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f268af36-3e9b-5883-8530-d4294ff1cee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161222" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d82b9d4-2d14-556e-bd39-2f19cc378d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194100} {:answer/id #uuid "be5d2076-63af-5548-9006-ebf5c675967b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194101} {:answer/id #uuid "eb06b31f-1ef5-55a7-924e-4e4ba53f1bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194102} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd67bc4-c1ee-5609-8dc9-f0423400fc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194103} {:answer/id #uuid "45ca0606-122e-5ef2-9732-097b64f4c9c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12π\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ca0606-122e-5ef2-9732-097b64f4c9c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12π\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194104} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5f5987-a252-5b77-8edb-2b3450b7b391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9383b6ea-48e1-59a7-921c-a6c0f41ef499" :question/math-academy-id "q-161232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/ea320b4b8a036bdf905a51041eb94ffb2464125af912ec74830d49af922f4342.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+y(y+2z)+z(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}-2yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c12b33-811e-508e-8645-0004d80a7b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356445} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1dec43-3890-5284-aa58-a559c521ca8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356446} {:answer/id #uuid "6474ca8e-aaf3-530b-a667-30bbfa2bbfd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356447} {:answer/id #uuid "c949b9d8-2c43-53ca-ac0c-ec0a2308649c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356448} {:answer/id #uuid "f7400176-8261-51ad-8ba2-c6617080999d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c949b9d8-2c43-53ca-ac0c-ec0a2308649c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356448} :answer-field/id #uuid "b207f87f-d52f-55c0-8a2c-c25837dedc4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21f27704-e307-5447-88bd-e468bb76c29c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(x(x-y)+y(y+x)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =108\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cb50c7-49fe-5553-a3ce-12a2bf22d44b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356452} {:answer/id #uuid "5042cd22-3988-5a32-9940-fb5213e002a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356453} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe95b20-71f0-58c8-af8a-173ed3169b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356454} {:answer/id #uuid "e8679a88-0fad-5d08-abca-df0360b03fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356455} {:answer/id #uuid "a79f135e-e81d-51c1-8af3-4d87812d6117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5042cd22-3988-5a32-9940-fb5213e002a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356453} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9eb6bdc-889f-50ae-9db4-3def4c21f884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61370059-3078-5e7b-a233-7ca4b50d9f37" :question/math-academy-id "q-161236" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-{z}^{2},\\,z(1+y)⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y-{z}^{2},\\,z(1+y)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y(y-{z}^{2})+{z}^{2}(1+y) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+{z}^{2}+y{z}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfddca3a-311f-5a8b-b80e-cfb258adf34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356459} {:answer/id #uuid "362c888a-848f-51a1-854c-010689550de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356460} {:answer/id #uuid "b82f879e-6e1b-5bbe-81da-e8b03ae38e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356461} {:answer/id #uuid "92741047-e974-5651-a563-720a95defe8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356462} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b03a95-4ad9-5dc9-8f52-4aea1f993faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfddca3a-311f-5a8b-b80e-cfb258adf34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356459} :answer-field/id #uuid "80318eb2-27e8-5ef5-b53c-bab4a09c7dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "458ea386-13da-5ecb-816f-36e185865cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161238" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/5f393842a0e53fcacf439250245935fe7a7ad068973ee5f896f11916c3881e57.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(x(x+xz)+{y}^{2}+z(z-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{x}^{2}z+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\  & =20\\pi \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1b360e-5964-50af-bd7d-954945ba7b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356466} {:answer/id #uuid "5edffc1c-606f-569d-9d2b-c605f4049869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356467} {:answer/id #uuid "97047edb-fa2d-5d24-87b6-c66922eaed6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356468} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c44cc4-e567-57b2-8bec-517997c14c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356469} {:answer/id #uuid "a2019e5f-1288-5a16-a602-1e77dd34c37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97047edb-fa2d-5d24-87b6-c66922eaed6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356468} :answer-field/id #uuid "b060f4a0-a27d-5af7-a8a0-b3b893c69ac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b03b044-5d71-5b5b-8c51-953fb03cf59a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161241" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc2478f-2b8d-55d1-912b-17eb2ab3a9ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194226} {:answer/id #uuid "0984f654-f5ca-5dd9-a8d3-95d2633d2f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194227} {:answer/id #uuid "169bca33-8afe-5598-9acf-79ad902953ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194228} {:answer/id #uuid "4a849ff6-2927-53b1-a9e4-c0477804729e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194229} {:answer/id #uuid "579e2588-50fb-588c-a482-799826eb7ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169bca33-8afe-5598-9acf-79ad902953ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194228} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e91d6af-a3ab-5fb2-a16e-c75f97a6992c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11b39dd0-c5da-564f-835c-b92be8cee48e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162550" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/fa7f40460a8834c73c61575aef821157e1aff7dace359752fd98593271812c10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/1f00f0e9fa8148ca3cf161322c926ea9fc16a4467991063cbdcb4f293254674d.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}({r}^{2}+4)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r({r}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}+2{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}+2) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,0({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =9\\pi +0 \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4314c74-82e8-58d1-ae65-02b96e9bc19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356473} {:answer/id #uuid "d8564fc0-1d94-519c-8eed-e33c7e25300e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356474} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9de8c-d171-5b0b-a6f6-5e565ebda64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356475} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ba1a5e-f930-5436-968e-ec83791f2625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356476} {:answer/id #uuid "2651a9d3-d6e5-59ad-924a-377b0c7a819f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9de8c-d171-5b0b-a6f6-5e565ebda64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356475} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e878cb-2673-5a04-b7eb-be87c08da50b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e6626d5-eaee-5da4-ade0-3b113a418809" :question/math-academy-id "q-162560" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/04f58e972500033523e3ca3247ea4e1c5c377d8dc2b402b1c0200d9c85529894.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8652a197f603c517cd462c69473e3a693ba8dcd76340b6241f890663356e00cb.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8a8cf76-d506-57c7-bd0c-731affcd12f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356480} {:answer/id #uuid "f93d7aec-d0cb-5b15-96a8-f046c7ee5371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356481} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6fd473-bedd-5469-a085-670ffa290c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356482} {:answer/id #uuid "28b40d93-db48-5737-8b9e-e2041f48512e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356483} {:answer/id #uuid "e1849de1-ee56-50aa-8739-fcb33e0ab643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8a8cf76-d506-57c7-bd0c-731affcd12f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356480} :answer-field/id #uuid "b13b1615-5a22-5eb1-9580-0bff283cced8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82cb2475-3100-5a72-830f-ebf6c4d02e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-162563" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/638a02dec15c2cecd8563854c2fb071ff70f10004f60339ef92d0914e7198ebc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/4fd228a9bf0a2d8382139d3f804ee7c9b3fd8721c8ad079de43e61b9e8947240.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =4+3{y}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(4+2z)y+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+2z+1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2z+5\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[{z}^{2}+5z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(3{y}^{2}+2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(3{y}^{2}+2z)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{y}^{3}+2yz]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[z+{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(3+9) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =24+(-12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
